1
00:00:00,478 --> 00:00:03,579
Hi everyone! Today I'm presenting IncidentLens,

2
00:00:04,068 --> 00:00:08,340
an evidence-driven multi-agent system for
software incident root cause analysis.

3
00:00:09,150 --> 00:00:14,080
In production postmortems, the most dangerous
failure mode isn't failing to find an answer —

4
00:00:14,080 --> 00:00:18,060
it's hallucinating a plausible
story. Generic LLM agents

5
00:00:18,060 --> 00:00:22,334
routinely assert confident root
causes without valid citations,

6
00:00:22,829 --> 00:00:27,291
ignore data access boundaries, and almost
never admit when evidence is missing.

7
00:00:27,503 --> 00:00:31,091
IncidentLens enforces a much
stricter engineering contract:

8
00:00:31,514 --> 00:00:35,220
every claim must cite an
actually observed evidence ID,

9
00:00:35,716 --> 00:00:39,120
every agent operates inside
explicit read-only tool permissions,

10
00:00:39,756 --> 00:00:43,451
and when visible telemetry cannot
uniquely identify the root cause,

11
00:00:43,931 --> 00:00:47,678
the system must abstain with
INCONCLUSIVE rather than guess.

12
00:00:48,086 --> 00:00:50,855
Over the next nine minutes,
I'll walk through the architecture,

13
00:00:51,241 --> 00:00:54,963
show you four live hands-on demos,
and share our full-scale,

14
00:00:55,219 --> 00:00:59,393
cross-model experimental findings
across DeepSeek and Tencent Hunyuan.

15
00:01:00,905 --> 00:01:05,717
Let's look at how IncidentLens works under the
hood. It runs as a centralized LangGraph workflow.

16
00:01:06,038 --> 00:01:09,987
Starting from a public incident summary, the
Coordinator inspects the current evidence gaps

17
00:01:09,987 --> 00:01:14,226
and remaining budget, then dynamically
dispatches one or two specialists per round:

18
00:01:14,679 --> 00:01:18,168
Logs for error chronology, Metrics
for capacity and dependencies,

19
00:01:18,629 --> 00:01:21,546
Database for locks, connections, and slow queries,

20
00:01:21,875 --> 00:01:26,684
and Changes for recent deployments and config
diffs. Two safeguards keep the workflow honest.

21
00:01:26,909 --> 00:01:30,587
First, strict tool ACL isolation—for example,

22
00:01:30,936 --> 00:01:36,188
the Logs agent can never query config diffs.
Second, a deterministic FinalizeGate:

23
00:01:36,615 --> 00:01:40,528
final acceptance never relies on
an LLM's self-reported confidence.

24
00:01:40,802 --> 00:01:45,349
Instead, deterministic code verifies
at least two independent sources,

25
00:01:45,908 --> 00:01:49,435
a cause-specific discriminator,
temporally compatible impact,

26
00:01:49,721 --> 00:01:52,840
and zero unresolved contradictions
or rival mechanisms.

27
00:01:53,181 --> 00:01:56,867
To evaluate whether dynamic multi-agent
orchestration actually pays off,

28
00:01:56,867 --> 00:02:02,367
we compare four architectures under
identical budgets and equivalent tool access:

29
00:02:02,815 --> 00:02:07,907
a Single-agent baseline, a one-shot Fixed-4
batch, our primary Dynamic architecture,

30
00:02:07,907 --> 00:02:11,955
and Full, which adds an
independent LLM Verifier loop.

31
00:02:13,279 --> 00:02:15,747
Let's jump straight into our live demonstrations.

32
00:02:16,546 --> 00:02:21,007
First, in the terminal, I run
validate-data to verify that

33
00:02:21,007 --> 00:02:25,965
public evidence files follow the strict
schema with zero leaked private answer fields.

34
00:02:26,068 --> 00:02:30,556
Next, let's verify our security
boundary with the isolation test suite.

35
00:02:31,095 --> 00:02:36,247
Notice how attempts by a specialist to
escalate permissions or call an unauthorized

36
00:02:36,247 --> 00:02:41,747
tool—like Logs trying to inspect config diffs —
are deterministically blocked at runtime.

37
00:02:42,414 --> 00:02:46,507
Running our broader offline regression
suite confirms 505 tests passing,

38
00:02:46,507 --> 00:02:50,427
alongside our 110-case provider
fault-injection matrix,

39
00:02:50,427 --> 00:02:52,957
which verifies bounded transport retries,

40
00:02:53,362 --> 00:02:56,338
atomic request permits, and
structured output recovery.

41
00:02:57,838 --> 00:03:00,681
Now let's run an actual live
investigation—not a mock.

42
00:03:01,153 --> 00:03:05,800
Here I invoke the CLI using
our newly integrated OhMyGPT

43
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:10,140
provider backed by the official
OpenAI SDK, calling Tencent

44
00:03:10,330 --> 00:03:15,539
hy4-flash with json_schema mode and thinking
disabled, under the dynamic architecture.

45
00:03:16,048 --> 00:03:20,556
This live run finishes with
INCONCLUSIVE and writes result.json,

46
00:03:20,901 --> 00:03:25,660
report.md, and trace.json. The report
preserves the leading explanations,

47
00:03:26,144 --> 00:03:30,152
observed evidence, and causal gaps.
A confirmed root cause remains null:

48
00:03:30,586 --> 00:03:34,986
the system does not turn a plausible
explanation into a claim it cannot verify.

49
00:03:35,686 --> 00:03:38,697
Next, let's look at two hardened benchmark cases

50
00:03:39,030 --> 00:03:43,104
we ran on official DeepSeek
deepseek-flash. On incident_4014,

51
00:03:43,373 --> 00:03:48,100
a misleading case where a non-causal change is
deliberately injected onto the culprit's own

52
00:03:48,100 --> 00:03:53,080
service, dynamic doesn't take the bait —
it cross-checks database lock waits and logs,

53
00:03:53,080 --> 00:03:57,773
and nails postgres_lock_contention
in 47 seconds. By contrast,

54
00:03:58,028 --> 00:04:02,794
on incident_4018, the critical slow-query
discriminator is missing by design.

55
00:04:03,277 --> 00:04:05,420
After checking 15 tools across sources,

56
00:04:05,811 --> 00:04:09,558
the system refuses to guess and
terminates cleanly with INCONCLUSIVE.

57
00:04:10,995 --> 00:04:13,745
To make every investigation
inspectable by human engineers,

58
00:04:14,085 --> 00:04:16,339
IncidentLens ships with a local Web Console.

59
00:04:16,777 --> 00:04:19,898
Here I open the Trace Archive at localhost:8022.

60
00:04:20,320 --> 00:04:24,311
Notice the banner at the top:
Recorded Replay—zero provider calls.

61
00:04:24,654 --> 00:04:29,962
Every view you're about to see is reconstructed
purely from the persisted trace.json.

62
00:04:30,484 --> 00:04:33,572
Let's inspect a RESOLVED run
first. In the timeline panel,

63
00:04:33,937 --> 00:04:38,376
we can step through every decision the
Coordinator made, which specialist it dispatched,

64
00:04:38,677 --> 00:04:42,349
and what exact records came back.
Switching to the hypothesis view,

65
00:04:42,625 --> 00:04:45,882
you can watch rival explanations
compete as new evidence arrives,

66
00:04:46,293 --> 00:04:49,413
until only one satisfies FinalizeGate.

67
00:04:49,795 --> 00:04:54,760
Clicking any citation in the final report traces
straight back to the underlying observed log,

68
00:04:54,949 --> 00:04:59,241
metric, or config diff—so reviewers
can verify grounding in seconds.

69
00:04:59,572 --> 00:05:03,513
At the bottom, the audit ledger records
the formal two-phase stop handshake —

70
00:05:03,513 --> 00:05:07,998
WORKFLOW_STOP_REQUESTED, STOP_ACK,
and NORMAL_TERMINATION —

71
00:05:08,300 --> 00:05:12,430
alongside exact physical HTTP
requests and token counts.

72
00:05:13,013 --> 00:05:16,414
Now let's switch to an abstained case.
Instead of crashing or guessing,

73
00:05:16,826 --> 00:05:21,693
the UI clearly shows INCONCLUSIVE,
explains which evidence gaps remain open,

74
00:05:21,693 --> 00:05:25,878
and distinguishes normal
abstention from a runtime error.

75
00:05:27,306 --> 00:05:30,637
Evaluating an agent rigorously
is just as hard as building it.

76
00:05:31,107 --> 00:05:35,715
IncidentBench physically separates public
evidence from private evaluator oracles;

77
00:05:36,227 --> 00:05:39,492
labels are only unlocked
after a run persists its state

78
00:05:39,780 --> 00:05:43,361
and generates a matching
SHA-256 completion receipt,

79
00:05:43,587 --> 00:05:46,246
and every technical failure
stays in the denominator.

80
00:05:46,516 --> 00:05:50,837
When we moved our project onto the official
DeepSeek API for full-scale testing,

81
00:05:51,148 --> 00:05:53,024
we uncovered a critical latent bug:

82
00:05:53,458 --> 00:05:57,860
Single and Full crashed on 8 out
of 12 slots during verification!

83
00:05:58,309 --> 00:06:01,072
Tracing the event logs
revealed a subtle hidden bug:

84
00:06:01,483 --> 00:06:05,565
ReviewOutput enforces a cross-field
rule called leading_review_consistency,

85
00:06:05,900 --> 00:06:09,247
but structured_output.py stripped
that custom error code into

86
00:06:09,247 --> 00:06:12,378
a generic $: validation_error,

87
00:06:12,886 --> 00:06:16,282
leaving the model completely blind
during its one repair attempt.

88
00:06:16,608 --> 00:06:21,108
After whitelisting the safe error code,
passing an explicit static repair hint,

89
00:06:21,526 --> 00:06:24,547
and documenting the invariant
in the verifier prompt —

90
00:06:24,547 --> 00:06:29,400
verified by this new unit test—all 9
previously crashed slots recovered 9 out of 9,

91
00:06:29,795 --> 00:06:32,445
and across our subsequent 144-slot full run,

92
00:06:32,812 --> 00:06:35,505
structured output crashes
dropped to literally zero.

93
00:06:37,040 --> 00:06:39,755
Now let's look at the numbers
from our full-scale experiments.

94
00:06:39,860 --> 00:06:44,080
On the 36-case hardened benchmark
with official deepseek-flash —

95
00:06:44,096 --> 00:06:49,244
144 slots across six difficulty levers —
Dynamic finishes first across the board:

96
00:06:49,696 --> 00:06:53,356
86.1% quality success (31 out of 36),

97
00:06:53,527 --> 00:06:58,412
cutting tokens by 42.6% compared to
Single and 39.5% compared to Full,

98
00:06:58,747 --> 00:07:02,977
while beating Fixed-4 18 to 0 in paired
comparisons. Even more remarkably,

99
00:07:03,212 --> 00:07:05,480
across all 137 completed runs,

100
00:07:05,853 --> 00:07:09,317
there were zero false resolutions —
whenever the system emitted RESOLVED,

101
00:07:09,592 --> 00:07:13,771
it was 100% right. To test whether these
findings generalize beyond DeepSeek,

102
00:07:14,168 --> 00:07:19,448
we ran the same six-lever benchmark
on Tencent hy4-flash via OhMyGPT,

103
00:07:20,023 --> 00:07:22,410
both with chain-of-thought
thinking enabled and disabled.

104
00:07:22,673 --> 00:07:26,840
Here we uncovered a counterintuitive result:
disabling thinking nearly doubled end-to-end

105
00:07:26,840 --> 00:07:31,080
success, cutting runtime from
48 minutes down to 12.6 minutes

106
00:07:31,080 --> 00:07:36,040
and lifting completion from 45.8% to 95.8%. Why?

107
00:07:36,040 --> 00:07:39,940
Because multi-agent RCA is a
serial tool-exploration loop,

108
00:07:39,940 --> 00:07:44,980
not a single-shot math puzzle. With thinking
enabled, each call took 40 to 70 seconds,

109
00:07:44,980 --> 00:07:49,510
causing Single and Full to exhaust the
900-second wall-clock budget mid-investigation.

110
00:07:50,020 --> 00:07:54,615
Disabling thinking let the workflow
trade slow internal monologues for fast,

111
00:07:54,778 --> 00:07:58,023
iterative evidence gathering —
guided externally by FinalizeGate.

112
00:07:58,560 --> 00:08:03,749
And look at the starkest finding of the
entire project: on non-thinking hy4-flash,

113
00:08:04,074 --> 00:08:08,310
the monolithic Single agent collapsed
to 0 out of 5 on identifiable cases,

114
00:08:08,508 --> 00:08:10,733
burning 254,000 tokens per run.

115
00:08:11,063 --> 00:08:15,300
Yet Dynamic multi-agent specialization
rescued accuracy from 16.7%

116
00:08:15,300 --> 00:08:19,368
all the way to 83.3%, while cutting
both tokens and latency in half!

117
00:08:19,875 --> 00:08:23,358
Finally, across both DeepSeek and hy4-flash,

118
00:08:23,717 --> 00:08:28,201
the one case Dynamic missed —
counterfactual pair incident_4035 —

119
00:08:28,588 --> 00:08:33,353
was successfully rescued by Full thanks to
the Verifier's targeted follow-up checks.

120
00:08:34,854 --> 00:08:40,240
To keep our evaluation scientifically honest,
we also have to highlight three clear limitations.

121
00:08:40,913 --> 00:08:44,640
First, achieving zero false
resolutions across both model

122
00:08:44,640 --> 00:08:48,749
families owes a great deal to the
tight coupling between FinalizeGate's

123
00:08:48,924 --> 00:08:52,850
seven hardcoded mechanism rules and
our synthetic benchmark scenarios.

124
00:08:53,213 --> 00:08:56,620
Testing on out-of-distribution,
unstructured production logs is

125
00:08:56,620 --> 00:09:00,607
essential to measure true hallucination
resistance when hard rules weaken.

126
00:09:00,911 --> 00:09:05,033
Second, our automatic scorer currently checks
culprit service and failure mode match,

127
00:09:05,471 --> 00:09:08,920
even though the oracle already defines
acceptable evidence support sets —

128
00:09:08,930 --> 00:09:12,920
incorporating support-set coverage and
re-centering paired bootstrap statistics

129
00:09:12,920 --> 00:09:16,740
onto Dynamic are immediate,
high-leverage upgrades. Third,

130
00:09:17,332 --> 00:09:22,553
our cross-model finding on incident_4035
points directly to an on-demand Verifier:

131
00:09:23,002 --> 00:09:26,960
keep Dynamic as the fast, economical
default, and trigger an independent

132
00:09:26,960 --> 00:09:31,217
Verifier only when competing-mechanism
or counterfactual signals appear.

133
00:09:32,763 --> 00:09:38,263
To wrap up: IncidentLens demonstrates that
combining scoped multi-agent specialization,

134
00:09:38,742 --> 00:09:42,660
deterministic grounding gates, and
failure-inclusive cross-model evaluation

135
00:09:42,660 --> 00:09:46,220
delivers zero-fabrication root cause analysis —

136
00:09:46,366 --> 00:09:50,184
not only saving 40% to 50%
in tokens on strong models,

137
00:09:50,648 --> 00:09:55,267
but dramatically amplifying diagnostic
capability on lighter, non-thinking models.

138
00:09:55,871 --> 00:09:59,188
Thank you so much for watching, and
I'd be happy to take any questions!
